\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A self-adjoint kernel with a nonalternating form}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Over #{\mathbb F_2}, take
##{B=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 K=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.}
The form #{B} is perfect and alternating. Matrix multiplication
gives
##{K^{\mathsf T}B=BK=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}.}
Thus #{K} is self-adjoint for #{B}, and #{BK} equals the
negative of its transpose. However, for the second basis vector,
#{B(e_2,Ke_2)=1}. The [kernel form](fdip-00UV) is not
alternating. A transpose identity alone therefore does not
supply the Pfaffian hypothesis in this characteristic.}
